@page "/how-it-works"

<PageTitle>How It Works - BasketElo</PageTitle>

<div class="info-page method-page">
    <header class="info-hero be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 0">
        <div class="info-hero-copy">
            <p class="info-kicker">Method</p>
            <h1>How the rating works.</h1>
            <p class="info-lede">A team starts at 1,500. After every game, its rating moves by an amount shaped by the opponent, the result, home court, and the score margin.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="info-facts" aria-label="Method at a glance">
            <div><dt>Starting rating</dt><dd>1,500</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Default model</dt><dd>Adjusted ELO</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Updated</dt><dd>After every result</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </header>

    <nav class="info-jump-links be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 1" aria-label="On this page">
        <span>On this page</span>
        <a href="#short-version">The short version</a>
        <a href="#models">The two models</a>
        <a href="#example">Worked example</a>
        <a href="#limits">What ELO misses</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="short-version" class="info-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 2" aria-labelledby="short-version-heading">
        <div class="info-section-label"><span>01</span><span>The short version</span></div>
        <div class="info-section-body">
            <h2 id="short-version-heading">Every result corrects an expectation.</h2>
            <p class="info-section-intro">Before a game, the rating gap becomes a win probability. After the game, the model compares that expectation with what actually happened.</p>
            <ol class="method-steps">
                <li><span>1</span><div><h3>Compare</h3><p>Higher-rated teams are expected to win more often. Home advantage is included before that probability is calculated.</p></div></li>
                <li><span>2</span><div><h3>Observe</h3><p>The final result—and, in the adjusted model, the score margin—shows how far reality was from the expectation.</p></div></li>
                <li><span>3</span><div><h3>Update</h3><p>The winner gains exactly what the loser gives up. Surprises move the ratings more than expected results.</p></div></li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="models" class="info-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 3" aria-labelledby="models-heading">
        <div class="info-section-label"><span>02</span><span>Two views</span></div>
        <div class="info-section-body">
            <h2 id="models-heading">Basic and Adjusted ELO answer slightly different questions.</h2>
            <div class="model-comparison">
                <article>
                    <div class="model-heading"><span>Baseline</span><h3>Basic ELO</h3></div>
                    <p>Looks only at who won, who lost, and how strong each team was beforehand. It is the clearest reference model.</p>
                    <ul><li>Win or loss</li><li>Opponent strength</li><li>No extra adjustments</li></ul>
                </article>
                <article class="is-default">
                    <div class="model-heading"><span>Used by default</span><h3>Adjusted ELO</h3></div>
                    <p>Adds the information in the final score. A convincing win can matter more than a narrow one, within a controlled limit.</p>
                    <ul><li>Everything in Basic</li><li>Score margin</li><li>Competition weight</li></ul>
                </article>
            </div>
            <p class="method-plain-note"><strong>“Default” simply means this is the model shown first in the public rankings.</strong> You can switch models there; their histories remain separate.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="example" class="info-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 4" aria-labelledby="example-heading">
        <div class="info-section-label"><span>03</span><span>Worked example</span></div>
        <div class="info-section-body">
            <h2 id="example-heading">A 1,600 team hosts a 1,500 team.</h2>
            <p class="info-section-intro">Using the current adjusted model, the calculation starts like this:</p>
            <div class="formula-example">
                <article><span>Effective gap</span><code>1,600 + 70 − 1,500 = 170</code><p>The home team receives a 70-point home-court adjustment.</p></article>
                <article><span>Win expectation</span><code>170 points → 73%</code><p>That gap makes the home team roughly a 73% favourite.</p></article>
                <article><span>Base update</span><code>20 × (1 − 0.73) = +5.4</code><p>An expected win earns a modest increase before the score-margin adjustment.</p></article>
            </div>
            <div class="method-constants" aria-label="Current model constants">
                <span><strong>20</strong> K-factor</span>
                <span><strong>70</strong> home advantage</span>
                <span><strong>400</strong> probability scale</span>
                <span><strong>28</strong> rating points per expected point</span>
            </div>
            <a class="info-text-link" href="model-lab">Test different assumptions in Model Lab <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="info-section info-section-last be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 5" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
        <div class="info-section-label"><span>04</span><span>Limits</span></div>
        <div class="info-section-body">
            <h2 id="limits-heading">A useful signal, not a verdict.</h2>
            <div class="method-limits">
                <p>ELO describes what past results support right now. It does not know about injuries, line-ups, travel, motivation, or a tactical change that has not appeared in the results yet.</p>
                <p>Every rating is tied to a versioned model, so an improved formula can be tested and rebuilt without silently rewriting an older history.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
